doi:10.14778/3718057.3718072and for which the models under-perform . However, data in practice
often misses to include demographic information of entities. Even in
cases where some demographic information are available, many
others may be missing that is necessary for identifying representa-
tion bias [ 38]. To further motivate this problem, let us consider the
following example:
Example 1. A data-sharing platform (e.g., Chicago Open Data
Portal [2]) would like to responsibly specify the under-represented
andunder-performing groups in their shared data in order to limit
their scope of use. However, their datasets either do not include
grouping attributes (such as race) or only contain some of those.
Moreover, targeting a comprehensive audit, they do not want to limit
their scope to a small set of predefined groups. Instead, they want
to be proactive in their detection process, even for the groups they
might have missed to consider.
For some cases such as image datasets, when a small and prede-
termined set of grouping attributes (e.g., gender ) are missing, one
may be able to use techniques such as crowdsourcing to identify
under-represented groups based on those attributes [ 31]. Our prob-
lem, however, is that the grouping attributes are not predetermined.
In other words, we consider an unknown unknown situation where
not only do we lack access to the grouping attributes but we also
do not know the groups of interest.
To address this problem, we introduce the problem of mining the
potential groups that are under-represented and under-performing,
when the grouping attributes are unknown. We refer to this prob-
lem as Minoria mining . To the best of our knowledge, this paper
isthe first to introduce and study the Minoria mining problem . In
simple terms, the main question we want to answer in this paper
is, “are there regions in data-space, representing potential
groups that are (a) under-represented, for which (b) the model
under-performs ?” For instance, aligned with Example 1, our experi-
ments on a Crimes dataset (§ 7.1.1) identified North-side Chicago as
under-represented and under-performing. This information helps
the model developers and data owners such as Chicago Open Data
Portal to proactively identify a potential issue, decide if it is socially
concerning, and limit the dataset’s scope-of-use accordingly.
Similar to any other data mining problem, multiple approaches
may come to mind for addressing this problem. For example, one
can adapt a clustering technique (such as k-means clustering [ 28]
or subspace and projective clustering [ 24,29,32]) to cluster the
data into potential groups. However, as we shall show in our experi-
ments (§ 7.1.2) such an approach fails to identify under-represented
groups, simply because there are not enough instances from those
groups in the dataset. This is a well-known drawback of the clus-
tering methods, that their performance degrades substantially for
detecting small clusters with insufficient data points [34].arXiv:2411.04761v2  [cs.LG]  21 Apr 2025

