6.2 Exploration and Exploitation (E&E)
The E&E heuristic views the problem as a reinforcement learning
instance and adapts the exploration-exploitation techniques for
finding a set of high-skew directions. In particular, our goal is to
select the next direction to evaluate sequentially to balance the
explore-exploit trade-off. That is, starting with a small set of ran-
domly selected directions, at each iteration, we either explore the
space of possible directions or exploit the best possible directions
found so far.
Without loss of generality, we adapt the epsilon-greedy ap-
proach [ 42]. Define𝜖≤1as the exploration probability. We flip a
coin with probability 𝜖in each iteration and choose to explore or
exploit based on this trial result. Let 𝑓′be the best-known solution
with the maximum skew so far while considering a hyper-parameter
𝜏′for the cosine similarity of the exploitation phase. During each
exploration step, we draw random directions in the space of all
possible directions. On the other hand, during the exploitation,
we select the best-known direction 𝑓′and select a random direc-
tion from the space of possible direction in the 𝜏-vicinity of 𝑓′,
defined as{𝑓(𝑠)|𝑐𝑜𝑠(𝑓′,𝑓(𝑠))≤𝜏′}. This problem can be reduced
to the problem of sampling uniformly from the surface of a unit
𝑑-dimensional spherical cone with the center 𝑓′and the angular
radius of𝜏. We use the sampling approach proposed in [8].
6.3 Focused Exploration
A significant challenge for the E&E approach is that in high dimen-
sional cases where 𝑑≫2, the search space becomes huge, making
it unlikely to find near-optimal directions. Therefore, instead, we
first identify the under-performing regions with the hope of reduc-
ing the search space significantly. To do so, we use the model ℎ𝜃,
trained onD, to find a subset 𝑆⊂D , which we call the error region ,
for whichℎ𝜃under-performs. Formally, the expected loss of the
model in𝑆is significantly higher compared to D\𝑆.
After finding the error region, we focus on the directions that
separate the error region 𝑆from other pointsD\𝑆. In other words,
