7.1.2 College Admissions. The College-admission dataset [ 3] con-
tains details about a group of students who sent applications to US
universities. This dataset has 400 rows and 7 columns indicating the
features of the submitted applications (like GRE, GPA, Gender/Race,
etc.). The female-to-male ratio in this dataset is around 1.1.
Prediction Task. The prediction target is whether an application
will result in an admission. We used a Logistic Regression model
for this binary classification task. It gives us an accuracy of 0.70
and an f1-score of 0.36. We removed the sensitive attribute Gender
from the dataset while training this classification model and never
used Gender in any implementation steps.
Hyper-parameters. We run the 2D Ray-sweeping algorithm on
the two columns GRE and GPA. We followed the same process
explained for the Chicago crime dataset and investigated the tail of
the highest-skew direction.
Results. In Fig 5a, we provide a visualization of GRE and GPA
values from this dataset. The red line shows the highest skew direc-
tion. Fig 5b visualizes the tail of this high-skew direction (percentile
𝑝=0.1). As we can see in Table 3, the ratio of the Female group is
significantly larger in the 0.1,0.08, and 0.04percentiles compared
to the whole dataset (1.1). The accuracy decreases up to %10 per-
cent when concentrating on the tail of this direction. In this case,
the Female group is not under-represented in the dataset because
the ratio is almost 1.0. However, it is an under-performing and
unknown minority group.
Runtime. The running time of the Ray-sweeping algorithm on
the Chicago Crime dataset in our settings was 8seconds. This time
was 3.74seconds for College-admission dataset.
7.1.3 Comparison with Baselines. We utilized two clustering tech-
niques as baselines: k-means [ 28] and Affinity Propagation [ 23].
The resulting clusters were analyzed to identify potential candi-
dates for the underrepresented demographic groups (Minoria). We
employed k-means with various values of 𝑘and utilized Affinity
Propagation with a carefully adjusted preference parameter to
control the number of clusters and avoid excessive clustering. Avisualization of the k-means clusters on College-admission dataset
is provided in Figure 5c.
The ratio of minority groups within each cluster in k-means for
various datasets is presented in Table 4. As reflected in the table,
unlike the region discovered using our methods (tail of skews),
the ratios of minority groups within each cluster are not signifi-
cantly different from the whole dataset (last row). As a result, these
clusters are not good candidates for identifying underrepresented
groups within the data. Furthermore, the number of points in each
cluster is significantly larger compared to the regions we identified,
making it more challenging to detect and analyze minority groups
within such clusters. In contrast, the regions we discovered are
more compact and therefore more efficient for mining underrepre-
sented groups, offering a practical and focused approach to this task.
Table 5 compares the results for various values of 𝑘, all yielding
consistent results.
The results of applying Affinity Propagation clustering are pre-
sented in Figure 7b. Similar challenges persist, including an ex-
cessive number of clusters, requiring all clusters to be examined.
Furthermore, the cluster ratios do not significantly differ from those
observed in the entire dataset.
Runtime. The average run-time of k-means on the College ad-
mission dataset in our setting is 0.003milliseconds. This value for
the Affinity Propagation method is 0.13seconds. Even though the
running time is smaller for clustering methods compared to the
Ray-sweeping algorithm, their output clusters are not valid for
solving the Minoria Mining problem defined in this work.
7.2 Higher-dimensional Experiments
In this section, we evaluate the higher-dimensional methods pro-
posed in § 6. As reflected in our negative result on the error-bound
of the discretization approaches [ 16], their effectiveness depends
on the dataset topology. Besides, their running time significantly in-
creases by the number of dimensions. Our experiments verify these
findings: we found discretization slow and less effective. There-
fore, in the following we focus on the more practical approaches
based on the search space exploration. Comparing the E&E method
(§ 6.2) and the focused exploration approach (§ 6.3), our experiments
found the latter (focused exploration) significantly more effective
for Minoria mining. Therefore, in the following, we first present our
results for this approach and then present our extended experiment
results for the E&E approach and highlight some of its drawbacks.
7.2.1 Focused Exploration. We ran experiments on three real-world
datasets. The Adult Income Dataset [ 20] consists of demographic
information for individuals, from the 1994 Census database. The
dataset is primarily used to predict whether a person earns more
than $50,000 per year based on various attributes (14 columns and
48,842 rows). Gender is the sensitive attribute in this dataset. The
COMPAS dataset [ 7] contains data used to evaluate the effective-
ness and fairness of the COMPAS recidivism prediction algorithm.
This dataset includes criminal history, jail and prison time, demo-
graphics, and COMPAS risk scores for defendants from Broward
County, Florida (52 columns and 18,316 rows). Race is the sensitive
attribute in this dataset. The last dataset is the Diabetes dataset [ 40].
This is a well-known dataset used for binary classification tasks
in the medical domain. It was collected by the National Institute
of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases and is available

