learning phase) are not linearly separable. The red points belong to
the minor group.These methods operate at a higher level of abstraction, focusing on
identifying high-skew directions. Our findings demonstrate that
these approaches, even though just heuristic, outperform traditional
clustering methods in finding problematic regions within datasets.
10 CONCLUSION
In this paper, we introduced, formalized, and studied the problem of
mining the unknown, underrepresented, and underperforming mi-
nority groups (Minoria). Making the connection to computational
geometry concepts, we proposed efficient algorithms and practical
solutions for finding high-skew projections as the combination of
data features that reflect high-performance disparities at their tail.
The scope of our proposed techniques is limited to the cases where
the Minoria are linearly separable from the rest of the data. Extend-
ing our work to the more general class of non-linear projections,
as well as developing alternative Minoria mining approaches, is an
interesting direction for future work.

